config BR2_PACKAGE_LTUNIFY
	bool "ltunify"
	select BR2_PACKAGE_LIBEXECINFO if !BR2_TOOLCHAIN_USES_GLIBC
	help
	  ltunify: pair, unpair and inspect devices on a Logitech
	  Unifying receiver, from the command line. It talks to the
	  receiver directly over /dev/hidraw*, writing HID++ 1.0
	  registers, and links against nothing but libc (+ -lrt).

	    ltunify list            show the six pairing slots
	    ltunify pair [timeout]  open the receiver for pairing
	    ltunify unpair idx      free a slot
	    ltunify info idx        per-device detail
	    ltunify receiver-info   receiver firmware/serial

	  WHY IT IS HERE. Nothing else on this image can re-pair a
	  Logitech device to a receiver. The kernel side is complete --
	  CONFIG_HID_LOGITECH_DJ gives each paired device its own input
	  node, and CONFIG_HID_LOGITECH_HIDPP its battery -- but the
	  pairing handshake itself has no kernel interface and no
	  sysfs knob. A device that arrives pre-paired in the box needs
	  none of this; a replacement receiver, a bare dongle off eBay,
	  or two devices being consolidated onto one dongle do.

	  WHAT IT CANNOT DO. Only UNIFYING and NANO receivers:

	    046d:c52b, c532   Unifying          supported
	    046d:c52f, c534   Nano              supported
	    046d:c548         Bolt              NOT supported
	    046d:c539, c53a,
	         c53f, c543   Lightspeed        NOT supported

	  Bolt uses a different pairing protocol, and hid-logitech-dj
	  binds it as "logitech-djreceiver" just like a Unifying one --
	  so ltunify's own auto-detection will select a Bolt receiver
	  and then fail against it. It also assumes the FIRST receiver
	  it finds is the one you meant, which is wrong precisely when
	  two are plugged in.

	  Both hazards are why this image does not expect anyone to run
	  ltunify by hand. /usr/sbin/mister-pair-logitech enumerates
	  every receiver, classifies it by USB product ID, refuses the
	  unsupported ones by name, and pins the chosen one with
	  ltunify's -d flag. Scripts/pair_logitech.sh on the data
	  partition launches it from the MiSTer Scripts menu.

	  Nothing invokes ltunify automatically: no init script, no
	  udev rule. Pairing is a deliberate act.

	  Upstream has been frozen since 2020-06-14. Accepted -- see
	  package/ltunify/ltunify.mk and docs/logitech-pairing.md,
	  which also record why Solaar (maintained, covers Bolt) is
	  not the choice here: it would pull GTK3 onto an image with
	  no display server.

	  https://github.com/Lekensteyn/ltunify
